Republic of the Philippines DEPARTML,. OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GO v ERNMENT DII,G-NAPOLCOM Center, EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, ZON CITY dilg.gov. ph OFFICE OF THE UNDERSECRETARY FOR LOCAL GOVERNMENT HON. RICARTE R. PADILLA Municipal Mayor Municipality of Jose Panganiban Province of Camarines Norte Dear Mayor Padilla: DILG Legal Opinion No. 31, S. 2013 30 This has reference to your letter dated 01 October 2013 asking for a legal opinion on the powers of the local chief executive (LCE), like you, to order for a deferment of an Order from a quasi-judicial body citing possible disruption of the peace and order therein as well as the general welfare of your constituents who may in the crossfire in case violence occurs in the implementation of the Order. Likewise, you are asking the clear mandate of the Supervisory Office for Security and Investigation Agencies (SOSIA) vis-å- vis the mandate of the local government unit (LGU) or the LCE under Republic Act No. 7160 (Local Government Code of 1991). While it may be true that Municipal Mayors, like you, have the power to maintain peace and order within your respective territorial jurisdictions, as exemplified in the following provisions of law: Section 16, Republic Act No. 7160 "SECTION 16. General Welfare. —Every local government unit shall exercise the powers expressly granted, those necessarily implied therefrom, as well as powers necessary, appropriate, or incidental for its efficient and effective governance, and those which are essential to the prom otion of the genera/ welfare. Within their respective territorial jurisdictions, local government units shall ensure support, among others, xxx maintain peace and order xxx. ' Section 444 (2)(vi), Republic Act No. 7160 (vi) Call upon the appropriate la w enforcement agencies to suppress disorder, riot, lawless violence, rebellion or sedition or to apprehend violators of the law when public interest so requires and the municipal police forces are inadequate to cope with the situation or the violators;

Section 51 of RA 6975, as amended by Section 62 of RA 8551 'Section 51. Powers of Local Government Officials Over the PNP Units or Forces. Governors and mayors shall be deputized as representatives of the Commission in their respective territorial jurisdiction. As such, the local executives shall discharge the follovving functions: (b) City and Municipal Mayors — (l) Operational Supervision and Control. The city and municipal mayors shall Mercise operational supervision and control over PNP units in their respective jurisdiction except during the thirty (30) day period immediately preceding and the thirty (30) days following any national, local and barangay elections. During the said period, the local police forces shall be under the supervision and control of the Commission on Elections. "The term bperational supervision and control' shall mean the power to direct, superintend, and oversee the day-to-day functions of police investigation of crime, crime prevention activities, and traffic control in accordance with the rules and regulations promulgated by the Commission. "It shall also include the power to direct the employment and deployment of units or elements of the PNP, through the station commander, to ensure public safety and effective maintenance of peace and order within the localitv. For this purpose, the terms employment' and deployment ' shall mean as follows: "'Employment' refers to the utilization of units or elements of the PNP for purposes of protection of lives and properties, enforcement of laws, maintenance of peace and order, prevention of crimes, arrest of criminal offenders and bringing the offenders to justice, and ensuring public safety, particularly in the suppression of disorders, riots, lawlessness, violence, rebellious and seditious conspiracy, insurgency, subversion or other related activities. '"Deployment' shall mean the orderly and organized physical movement of elements or units of the PNP within the province, city or municipality for purposes of employment as herein defined. " it is however equally true that you have also the concomitant duty to faithfully discharge Y'our duties and functions as provided by law. Considering such duty, an LCE had to enforce decisions or final resolutions, orders or rulings of courts and quasi-judicial bodiesl. Velasco vs. The Sandiganbayan and the People of the Philippines, G.R. No. 160991, 28 February 2013.

As to the request for this Department to render a categorical opinion on the deferment on the implementation of the SOSIA Order in relati<n to the foregoing power of a Mayor to maintain peace and order within his territorial jurisdiction, we regret to inform you that we are constrained in the meantime from rendering the same. Per information from SOSIA, they have already received a similar request from you and that they are already in the process of preparing their response. Considering that the Order involved came from their Office, we shall let SOSIA give its response before we take further action thereon. Thank you. Ls,'17 Copy furnished: PDG ALAN LA MADRID PURISIMA Chief, PNP Camp Crame, Quezon City PCSUPT TOMAS G. RENTOY 111, CESO VI Chief. SOSIA RD BLANDINO M. MACEDA DILG Region V Rizal St., Legazpi City Very truly yours, AUST RE A. PANADERO Undersecretary DUG.OUSLG
